WEBVTT

00:00:10.653 --> 00:00:15.743
Now we're looking at the options for handling
our on-line orders. They're going through

00:00:15.743 --> 00:00:22.563
the roof and frankly the lead-time for delivery
is blowing out. We need to improve our performance

00:00:22.563 --> 00:00:25.883
in this area. Any suggestions?

00:00:25.883 --> 00:00:30.583
Well, as I see it, we have three options.
The obvious one is to employ more people to

00:00:30.583 --> 00:00:36.163
do the job. Another alternative is to automate
the system more - cut down on the physical

00:00:36.163 --> 00:00:37.443
handling.

00:00:37.443 --> 00:00:39.653
And the third option?

00:00:39.653 --> 00:00:41.642
We could outsource.

00:00:41.642 --> 00:00:43.473
What are the pros and cons?

00:00:43.473 --> 00:00:49.793
Well, looking at increasing staff versus automation,
we have to consider the cost. Automating has

00:00:49.793 --> 00:00:55.254
a higher capital cost than putting on more
staff. On the other hand, employing more people

00:00:55.254 --> 00:01:00.213
is more expensive over a long term. If we
keep growing, it'll cost more in the long

00:01:00.213 --> 00:01:01.363
run.

00:01:01.363 --> 00:01:05.293
How likely is it that we'll see continued
growth?

00:01:05.293 --> 00:01:07.963
I'd say it's a certainty.

00:01:07.963 --> 00:01:11.103
I'd say a high probability. Nothing's certain
in business.

00:01:11.103 --> 00:01:13.293
So what about the third option?

00:01:13.293 --> 00:01:18.263
Outsourcing? Well, it does take the problem
off our hands. But we lose contact with our

00:01:18.263 --> 00:01:19.263
customers.

00:01:19.263 --> 00:01:21.123
What about the bottom line?

00:01:21.123 --> 00:01:26.423
Outsourcing is the cheapest option, and the
easiest - in the short term. But if we want

00:01:26.423 --> 00:01:33.053
to keep the operation in-house, the best option
is automating our system. The only down side

00:01:33.053 --> 00:01:36.592
is, we're taking a risk that our business
will keep growing.

00:01:36.592 --> 00:01:38.043
Which we hope it will.

00:01:38.043 --> 00:01:50.433
We certainly do.
We've looked before at formal meetings. Today's

00:01:50.433 --> 00:01:57.863
meeting is a more informal one, to discuss
a specific issue. The discussion is more free-flowing,

00:01:57.863 --> 00:02:05.273
or uncontrolled. Let's look first at some
of the language used by Denise when she introduces

00:02:05.273 --> 00:02:09.553
the problem.
Now we're looking at the options for handling

00:02:09.553 --> 00:02:15.133
our on-line orders. They're going through
the roof and frankly the lead-time for delivery

00:02:15.133 --> 00:02:20.343
is blowing out. We need to improve our performance
in this area.

00:02:20.343 --> 00:02:24.203
Denise says, "We're looking at the options."

00:02:24.203 --> 00:02:31.903
Options are different solutions, or answers,
to a problem. What is the problem? On-line

00:02:31.903 --> 00:02:35.392
orders are going through the roof.

00:02:35.392 --> 00:02:40.303
On-line orders are orders for goods received
through the internet, and if they're going

00:02:40.303 --> 00:02:43.883
through the roof, they are increasing in number
very rapidly.

00:02:43.883 --> 00:02:51.063
The lead-time for delivery is the amount of
time it takes from when the order is received

00:02:51.063 --> 00:02:58.903
to when it's delivered, and if it's blowing
out - that time is becoming too long. We use

00:02:58.903 --> 00:03:06.013
the expression blowing out for something which
is becoming too great, in a bad way.

00:03:06.013 --> 00:03:12.172
So to improve our performance means, in this
case, to shorten the time it takes to deliver

00:03:12.172 --> 00:03:14.193
goods.

00:03:14.193 --> 00:03:20.282
Let's look at Tan's suggested options.
Well, as I see it, we have three options.

00:03:20.282 --> 00:03:26.222
The obvious one is to employ more people to
do the job. Another alternative is to automate

00:03:26.222 --> 00:03:29.443
the system more - cut down on the physical
handling.

00:03:29.443 --> 00:03:31.612
And the third option?

00:03:31.612 --> 00:03:37.612
We could outsource.
Tan describes three options. First he lets

00:03:37.612 --> 00:03:42.903
us know that this is his opinion, by saying,
"As I see it."

00:03:42.903 --> 00:03:48.833
Practise with Tan some different ways of letting
someone know that what you're stating is your

00:03:48.833 --> 00:03:56.043
opinion.
As I see it, there are three options.

00:03:56.043 --> 00:04:01.733
In my opinion there are three options.

00:04:01.733 --> 00:04:07.422
From my point of view there are three options.

00:04:07.422 --> 00:04:15.263
As far as I'm concerned, there are three options.
The three options are: employ more people,

00:04:15.263 --> 00:04:18.833
automate, and outsource.

00:04:18.833 --> 00:04:26.093
To outsource means to use an outside company.
When presenting different options, we can

00:04:26.093 --> 00:04:35.113
order them by numbers, like this. Firstly,
we could employ more people, secondly we could

00:04:35.113 --> 00:04:41.233
automate, and thirdly we could outsource.

00:04:41.233 --> 00:04:51.363
We can also use phrases, such as 'one option
is to' and 'another option is to'.

00:04:51.363 --> 00:04:58.093
We can also use linking words, such as 'or'
and 'alternatively'.

00:04:58.093 --> 00:05:05.553
Or, we can use a combination of these methods.

00:05:05.553 --> 00:05:09.903
Now let's look at the language used to discuss
these options.

00:05:09.903 --> 00:05:16.183
Well, looking at increasing staff versus automation,
we have to consider the cost. Automating has

00:05:16.183 --> 00:05:21.654
a higher capital cost than putting on more
staff. On the other hand, employing more people

00:05:21.654 --> 00:05:27.473
is more expensive over a long term.
When considering two options, we are comparing

00:05:27.473 --> 00:05:35.373
them. Barbara talks about increasing staff
versus automation. She is saying that she

00:05:35.373 --> 00:05:44.123
is going to compare these two things. Another
phrase she could use is 'as against'. Practise

00:05:44.123 --> 00:05:53.572
with her.
Let's look at increased staff versus automation.

00:05:53.572 --> 00:06:04.603
Let's look at increased staff as against automation.
When comparing two things, we use comparative

00:06:04.603 --> 00:06:05.603
adjectives.

00:06:05.603 --> 00:06:12.203
Listen to Barbara again, and see if you can
hear the two comparative adjectives.

00:06:12.203 --> 00:06:17.352
Automating has a higher capital cost than
putting on more staff. On the other hand,

00:06:17.352 --> 00:06:21.143
employing more people is more expensive over
a long term.

00:06:21.143 --> 00:06:28.933
She says automating has a higher capital cost
than putting on more staff. Higher is a comparative

00:06:28.933 --> 00:06:31.483
adjective.

00:06:31.483 --> 00:06:38.352
We often use 'than' for the option that is
being compared. Remember for words of longer

00:06:38.352 --> 00:06:45.912
than two syllables, we use 'more' for the
comparative. Employing more people is more

00:06:45.912 --> 00:06:52.363
expensive. Because Barbara has already said
what the second option is, automating, she

00:06:52.363 --> 00:06:59.153
doesn't need to say employing more people
is more expensive than automating .

00:06:59.153 --> 00:07:05.292
Notice that she uses the phrase 'on the other
hand'. This is used to introduce another side

00:07:05.292 --> 00:07:15.553
to an argument. Practise this with Barbara.
On the one hand automation is expensive.

00:07:15.553 --> 00:07:23.833
On the other hand it's more efficient.
Another way of comparing two ideas is to use

00:07:23.833 --> 00:07:30.033
linking words such as but , although and however
.

00:07:30.033 --> 00:07:37.882
Automation is expensive, but it's more efficient.

00:07:37.882 --> 00:07:46.403
Although automation is expensive, it's more
efficient.

00:07:46.403 --> 00:07:53.132
Automation is expensive, however it's more
efficient.

00:07:53.132 --> 00:07:58.513
Now listen to the discussion about the likelihood
of continued growth.

00:07:58.513 --> 00:08:02.453
How likely is it that we'll see continued
growth?

00:08:02.453 --> 00:08:05.123
I'd say it's a certainty.

00:08:05.123 --> 00:08:08.393
I'd say a high probability. Nothing's certain
in business.

00:08:08.393 --> 00:08:15.673
Denise asks how likely continued growth is.
In looking at words to describe likelihood,

00:08:15.673 --> 00:08:20.343
we can use these words:
unlikely

00:08:20.343 --> 00:08:22.162
possible
probable

00:08:22.162 --> 00:08:25.663
certain

00:08:25.663 --> 00:08:29.372
So we can say:
It's unlikely

00:08:29.372 --> 00:08:34.553
It's possible
And so on.

00:08:34.553 --> 00:08:40.491
We can also qualify these with words such
as: very, quite,

00:08:40.491 --> 00:08:43.991
highly or reasonably

00:08:43.991 --> 00:08:48.793
It' s very unlikely
It's quite possible

00:08:48.793 --> 00:08:54.963
Its highly probable
It's reasonably certain

00:08:54.963 --> 00:09:00.013
And in a different kind of sentence, we can
use them as nouns:

00:09:00.013 --> 00:09:04.383
It's a certainty
It's a possibility

00:09:04.383 --> 00:09:10.663
There's a probability
There's a high likelihood

00:09:10.663 --> 00:09:18.273
But we don't say, "There's an unlikelihood."
We say, "There's no likelihood."

00:09:18.273 --> 00:09:23.973
Finally, look at what happens when we compare
more than two options.

00:09:23.973 --> 00:09:29.253
Outsourcing is the cheapest option, and the
easiest, in the short term. But if we want

00:09:29.253 --> 00:09:33.963
to keep the operation in-house, the best option
is automating our system.

00:09:33.963 --> 00:09:38.953
Did you hear the superlative adjectives used
to compare more than two things.

00:09:38.953 --> 00:09:44.713
Listen again. There are three.
Outsourcing is the cheapest option, and the

00:09:44.713 --> 00:09:50.273
easiest, in the short term. But if we want
to keep the operation in-house, the best option

00:09:50.273 --> 00:09:54.422
is automating our system.
Well our bottom line is that that's all we

00:09:54.422 --> 00:10:00.083
have time for today so I hope it's quite certain
I'll see you next time for The Business of

00:10:00.083 --> 00:10:00.703
English.